* Roadmap

- **Why planning matters with agents**: code is now cheap and understanding is
  not, so the plan is where a human keeps control
- **Planning hierarchy**: from an idea to a commit, in four phases, each closed
  by a human gate
- **Plan documents**: the docs to start with, a PRD whose requirements a test
  can check, and the step from the PRD to the design
- **End-to-end plans**: one plan file, versions that run, PR entries, the order
  of the PRs, and the architecture rules applied while planning
- **Requirements and decisions**: capturing requirements with an agent, tracing
  them to PRs and tests, and recording the decisions made while planning
- **Tracking progress**: a progress log of facts, findings with evidence, and
  the loop that closes after each merge
- **Planning with an AI**: task lists, a queue of tasks, criticizing the plan
  before an agent executes it, and spec-driven tools

# Why Planning Matters With Agents

* Code Is Cheap, Understanding Is Not
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.A_proposal_for_AI_based_software_engineering.md:55 '### The New-AI Way of "Building Software"'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.A_proposal_for_AI_based_software_engineering.md:400 '## 5. Change the Workflow: Spec First, Code Second (Weeks 2–4)'

- @Problem@: an agent writes code faster than a human can read it
  - Writing code costs minutes
  - Understanding and verifying the code costs what it always did

- @Problem@: when generation outpaces comprehension, a team gets
  - PRs too large to review
  - Code that no human read in full
  - An architecture that erodes one "reasonable-looking" change at a time

// TODO(gp): Check the "30 lines versus 2,000 lines" figure: it is an estimate
// of the blog draft, not a measured number
- @Solution@: review the plan before the code exists
  - A plan of about 30 lines replaces a review of about 2,000 lines of diff
  - The plan is cheap to change, and a wrong diff is not

- @Key idea@: human understanding is the scarce resource, and planning is the
  step that protects it

* What an Agent Does Without a Plan
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:271 '* Automating Requirements Capture'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.My_agentic_engineering_flow.md:52 '## Criticize First, Then Execute'

// TODO(gp): Check the claim that an agent rarely stops to ask: it is recalled
// from experience, and the material only says that it fills the gap
- @Problem@: an agent that gets a one-line request fills the gap between
  _"what was asked"_ and _"what to build"_ with assumptions
  - It rarely stops to ask unless an instruction tells it to
  - A wrong assumption compounds in every commit downstream

// TODO(gp): Check the rate limit example: the open choices are an
// illustration, not taken from a source
- @Example@: _"add rate limiting to `shortlink`"_ leaves open
  - Per client IP or per endpoint? How many requests per minute?
  - In memory, or shared by all the processes of the service?
  - What does a blocked client get: a `429`, a delay, a ban?
  - Each open choice is a guess, and the PR builds on the guess

- @Comparison@:
  - _Without a plan_: the misunderstanding shows up in review, after the code
  - _With a plan_: the misunderstanding shows up in a 30-line doc, before the
    first line of code

- @Key idea@: the cheapest place to catch a misunderstanding is before the
  branch exists

* Humans Own Intent, Agents Own Implementation
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.A_proposal_for_AI_based_software_engineering.md:100 '## Principles'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:67 '## From Idea to Merged PR'

- @Definition@: **planning** turns an idea into an ordered set of small,
  reviewable PRs, each with a check that says when it is done

- @Components@: a split of the work between humans and agents
  - _Humans own_: the intent, the scope, the architecture, and the approval
  - _Agents own_: the drafts of the docs and the implementation, inside
    boundaries that a machine checks
  - _Both_: the agent drafts each doc, and a human reads, fixes, and approves it

- @Remark@: the same split holds for every doc in this lesson
  - A skill writes the PRD, the design, the plan, and the task list
  - A human closes each phase: an agent never crosses a gate alone

- @Key idea@: an agent can draft a plan in minutes, but only a human can decide
  that it is the right plan

* A Running Example: `shortlink`
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:20 '## What It Is'
