* Docs and Skills per Phase
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:326 '## Define'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:342 '## Plan'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:356 '## Build'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:443 '## Update Docs and Close GH Issue'

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Phase", "Doc written", "Skill that drafts it"),
  rows: (
    ("Define", raw("01-prd.md"), raw("project.write_prd")),
    ("", raw("02*-design.*.md"), raw("architecture.write_design")),
    ("Plan", raw("04-plan.md"), raw("project.create_plan")),
    ("", [#raw("05-tasks.md"), #raw("06-spec.*.md")], raw("auto_task.create_plan_for_feature")),
    ("Build", "Code, tests, PR", raw("auto_task.execute_*")),
    ("Close", [#raw("04-plan.md"), #raw("07-progress.md"), #raw("03-decisions.md")], raw("project.update_progress")),
  ),
  caption: "Each phase writes its docs with a skill, and a human reviews them",
  col-widths: (0.6fr, 1.8fr, 2.2fr),
)
```

- @Key idea@: each skill reads the doc of the phase before, so a stale doc
  upstream misleads every step downstream

# Plan Documents

* Start With Three Docs
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:21 '## Use One Doc per Question'
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:241 '## Write the Meta-Doc'

- @Problem@: a full doc set for a two-week project is overhead, not planning

- @Solution@: start with three docs and add the others when the project needs
  them
  - `04-plan.md`: what to build, and in which order
  - `07-progress.md`: what is done, learned, and next
  - `08-how_we_work.md`: how the team works on this project

- @Definition@: the **meta-doc** `08-how_we_work.md` tells a newcomer what this
  project adds to the shared process
  - Test: after reading it, a newcomer delivers work of the same quality as the
    current contributors
  - It points to the shared docs instead of repeating them

- @Remark@: the doc set, the slot numbers, and the stable ids are covered in
  detail in Lesson 08.01
  - Add the PRD and the design docs when the scope or the code is too large to
    keep in one plan

* Writing a PRD
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:147 '## Write a PRD (Product Requirements Document)'
// From: .claude/skills/project.write_prd/SKILL.md:25 '## Ask If the Idea Is Unclear'

- @Definition@: a **product requirements document** (PRD) says what the product
  must do and must not do, and never how it is built

- @Components@: the sections of `01-prd.md`
  - _Goal_ and _Users and use cases_
  - _Requirements_: one H3 `` ### `R<N>` (<title>) `` per requirement, grouped
    by H2
  - _Non-goals_, _Success metrics_, _Constraints and assumptions_
  - _Open questions_: numbered, with `-> Project` on those that need their own
    project

- @Techniques@: `/project.write_prd <idea>` drafts the PRD
  - If the goal, the users, or the scope is unclear, it asks and waits
  - It never guesses a requirement to fill a section: an unresolved item goes
    to _Open questions_

- @Key idea@: a PRD with a design inside it fixes the solution before anyone
  looked at the alternatives

* Requirements That a Test Can Check
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:20 '## What It Is'

- @Problem@: a requirement that no test can check is a wish, and an agent
  implements a wish in any way it likes

- @Comparison@:
  - _Bad_: _"alias conflicts are handled gracefully"_
  - _Good_: `R3`: _"when a user asks for an alias that already exists,
    `POST /links` returns `409`, and the existing link still redirects to its
    URL"_
  - _Bad_: _"the service resists abuse"_
  - _Good_: `R7`: _"a client that sends more than 10 `POST /links` in one
    minute gets `429` with a `Retry-After` header, and other clients still
    succeed"_

- @Intuition@: each good requirement names an input, an observable output, and
  a condition, so the test writes itself

- @Key idea@: if you cannot write the test, you cannot review the PR

* Non-Goals and Success Metrics
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:20 '## What It Is'

- @Motivation@: an agent extends the scope whenever the scope is silent
  - A non-goal is a requirement that says _"stop here"_

- @Examples@: three non-goals of the `shortlink` features
  - No user accounts: the rate limit keys on the client IP
  - No check of a target URL against a list of phishing sites
  - No tracking of single visitors: analytics count clicks per link and day

- @Examples@: its three success metrics
  - _Redirect latency_: with analytics on, the p95 of `GET /{code}` stays
    under 10 ms, on a replay of 10,000 requests
  - _Click accuracy_: the stats endpoint matches a replay of 10,000 clicks
    within 1%
  - _Flood containment_: 1,000 `POST /links` from one client create at most 10
    links per minute, and a second client sees no `429`

- @Remark@: a success metric becomes a version of the plan: `v0.4` exists only
  to measure these three numbers

* From the PRD to the Design
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.write_design/SKILL.md:32 '## Split Into Components'
// From: .claude/skills/architecture.write_design/SKILL.md:38 '## Write the Overview'

- @Goal@: decide how to build the product before the code exists

- @Algorithm@: `/architecture.write_design <PRD>`
  1. Read the PRD, the existing code, and the architecture rules
  2. Ask the user if a requirement is ambiguous or two requirements conflict
  3. Propose components, each with one responsibility, and wait for the user
  4. Write `02-design.architecture.md` with a C4 diagram, mapping each
     requirement to a component
  5. Write one `02.<N>-design.<component>.md` per component
  6. Record each non-obvious choice in `03-decisions.md`

- @Example@: the design of `shortlink` splits it into 5 components (API,
  service, storage, rate limiter, and analytics), each with its own design doc

- @Remark@: the design rules themselves are the topic of Lesson 13.01

# End-to-End Plans

* One Plan: Roadmap and Execution
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:255 '## Keep One Plan'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:230 '## Create an End-to-End Plan'

- @Goal@: one canonical file says what to build and in which order, so that
  anyone, human or agent, picks the next PR without asking

- @Definition@: `04-plan.md` is both
  - The **roadmap**: versions, and the PRs each version needs
  - The **execution plan**: the PRs of the next version, split into tasks with
    owners, dates, and acceptance criteria

- @Problem@: two plan files, one for the roadmap and one for the execution,
  drift apart within weeks

- @Remark@: write the execution part only when several people work in parallel
  - A solo developer with an agent needs only the roadmap and the PR entries

- @Techniques@: `/project.create_plan <design>` drafts the file, and creates
  `07-progress.md` if it does not exist

* The Structure of `04-plan.md`
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:264 '## Structure the Plan File'
// From: .claude/templates/project_plan.template.md:1 '# <Project> Implementation Plan'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=52%}
- @Components@: the sections, in order
  - _Goal_: what we build, with pointers to the PRD and the design, not a copy
  - _Plan hierarchy_: how the plan relates to the progress and decisions logs
  - _Roadmap_: one bullet per version, with the checkbox list of its PRs
  - _Component specs_: the goal and the open questions of each component
  - _PRs_: one section per version, then one _Unscheduled_ section per component
  - _Conventions_: pointers to the coding and testing rules

- @Remark@: `[x]` means merged, not written
  - A partly done PR keeps `[ ]` and states its status in its entry
::::
:::: {.column width=48%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```text
  # <Project> Plan
  ## Goal
  ## Plan Hierarchy
  ## Roadmap
  - v0.1: <demo>
    - [ ] PR_A1
  # Component Specs
  ## <Component>
  # PRs
  ## v0.1: <Title>
  ### PR_A1: [ ] <Title>
  ## Conventions
```
\endgroup

::::
:::

* Versions That Run
// From: .claude/skills/project.create_plan/SKILL.md:32 '## Split Into Versions'
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/running_example.md:20 '## What It Is'

- @Definition@: a **version** is a set of PRs whose result a person can run or
  demo
  - Releases are cumulative: a version needs the PRs of the versions before it

```graphviz[width=95%]
digraph Versions {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.30;
    ranksep=0.45;
    rankdir=LR;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.07",
          height=0.50];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75];

    // version : blue | measurement : green

    V1 [label=<<b>v0.1</b> (5 PRs)<br/>custom aliases>,
        fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    V2 [label=<<b>v0.2</b> (3 PRs)<br/>rate limiting>,
        fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    V3 [label=<<b>v0.3</b> (4 PRs)<br/>click analytics>,
        fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    V4 [label=<<b>v0.4</b> (2 PRs)<br/>measured success metrics>,
        fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];

    V1 -> V2 -> V3 -> V4;
}
```
label=fig:versions
caption=The four versions of the shortlink features: three deliver a feature that runs (blue), and the last one measures the PRD metrics (green)

- @Intuition@: each version ends on something a user can try, so feedback
  arrives after weeks, not after the whole project
  - `v0.1` already lets a user create `/my-talk` instead of a random code

- @Key idea@: order versions so each needs only the versions before it, and
  plan the next one in detail and the later ones as a few bullets

* Anatomy of a PR Entry
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:306 '## Format a PR Entry'
// From:

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}
- @Components@: one H3 per PR, with a stable id and a checkbox
  - _Background_: the current state and the gap
  - _Changes_: concrete and testable
  - _Unit tests_: the behavior to check, not _"add tests"_
  - _Result_: what a user can do after the PR
  - Links to its requirements and decisions

- @Definition@: the id is `PR_<component letter><number>`, e.g., `PR_T1`
  - Never reuse or renumber it
::::
:::: {.column width=55%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```text
  ### PR_T1: [ ] Shared Types
  - Background: v0 passes dicts
  - Changes:
    - Add shortlink_types.py
    - Add an import graph test
  - Decisions: D2
  - Spec: needed
  - Unit tests:
    - A field cannot be set
    - A bad import fails
  - Result: one data model
```
\endgroup

::::
:::

* Ordering the PRs
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.create_plan_for_feature/SKILL.md:90 '## Rank the PRs'
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:264 '## Structure the Plan File'
// From: helpers_root/docs/work_organization/all.scrum.explanation.md:89 '## High-priority vs lower-priority tasks'

- @Algorithm@: order the PRs of a version
  1. By dependency: a PR never comes before a PR it needs
  2. Among independent PRs, by increasing complexity, starting with the highest
     design confidence

- @Example@: `v0.1` starts with `PR_T1` (the shared types) and `PR_S1` (an
  `alias` column in storage), since later PRs use the types and the column,
  and ends with `PR_P1` (the `alias` field of `POST /links`), which calls the
  code built before it

- @Techniques@: keep the plan honest about the future
  - Write the PR entries of the next version in detail, with dependencies and
    complexity
  - Keep later versions as a few bullets until they are close
  - File GitHub issues only for the next version: issues for later versions go
    stale

- @Comparison@: the same rule as a Scrum backlog _(Lacey, 2012)_
  - High-priority items are small and clear, so they enter the next sprint
  - Low-priority items stay large and fuzzy until they are close

* Open Questions Stay in the Plan
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:264 '## Structure the Plan File'
// From:

- @Problem@: an open question that lives only in a chat thread is answered by
  the agent, with a guess, in the middle of a PR

- @Solution@: each component spec in the plan has numbered open questions
  - Tag with `-> Project` a question that needs its own project
  - A blocked PR names the question that blocks it

- @Example@: _Evaluation_ of the `shortlink` features
  - Open question 1: _"Which machine replays the 10,000 requests, and who
    records the traffic that it replays?"_
  - `PR_V1` and `PR_V2` say: _"Blocked by Evaluation open question 1"_, so all
    of `v0.4` waits for a person, not for code

- @Example@: an unscheduled PR answers an open question
  - `PR_L4` adds a limit on `GET /{code}`: _"Answers Rate limiter open
    question 1"_

- @Key idea@: the plan shows not only what to build, but also what nobody has
  decided yet

* Architecture Rules During Planning
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:173 '## Follow the Architecture Rules'

- @Goal@: every design, plan, and PR follows the same architectural decisions,
  so the code of different projects is organized the same way

- @Techniques@: apply the rules of `.claude/skills/architecture.rules.md` at
  each phase, and cite the tag of a rule instead of copying its text
  - _Define_: organize the code in layers (`ARCH1.1`), and split a sequential
    process into stages (`ARCH3.1`)
  - _Plan_: cut the PRs along the lines of the architecture, one per layer or
    per stage; put the data classes in the first PR; make each PR testable
    alone
  - _Review_: quote the tag in a comment, e.g., _"split this module by
    responsibility: `ARCH1.4`"_

- @Example@: `PR_T1` adds an import graph test, so a storage module that
  imports the service layer fails the tests from the first PR on

- @Remark@: a departure from a rule is a decision: record it in
  `03-decisions.md` and cite the tag it departs from

* When a PR Needs a Spec
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.create_plan_for_feature/SKILL.md:47 '## Decide Which PRs Need a Full Spec'
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:235 '## Write a Spec for a Complex PR'

- @Problem@: a PR entry of ten lines is too short for a PR that adds an
  interface, and a spec for a rename is waste

- @Definition@: a PR needs a full spec `06-spec.<PR_ID>.md` if it
  - Adds a new interface: a class, a function, a CLI flag
  - Makes a non-obvious design decision
  - Carries risk: shared code, many call sites, hard to reverse

- @Components@: a spec, under 100 lines
  - Design decisions, each against its alternatives
  - Out of scope, the current state of the code, the unit test plan, and the
    risks
  - Facts from reading the code, kept apart from the decisions

- @Techniques@: mark the PR with `Spec: needed` in the plan, and write the spec
  with `/auto_task.create_plan_for_feature` before the work starts

- @Remark@: a mechanical PR (a rename, a config change, a new test) needs only
  its plan entry

# Requirements and Decisions

* Automating Requirements Capture
// From: book_AI_Software_Engineering/lectures_source/Tools_source.smd:271 '* Automating Requirements Capture'
// From: .claude/skills/project.write_prd/SKILL.md:25 '## Ask If the Idea Is Unclear'

- @Definition@: a **specification** separates _"what the user wants"_ from
  _"how it is built"_, and is written before a branch exists

- @Solution@: a skill drafts the spec from a request
  - Scope, interface, edge cases, and what is explicitly out of scope
  - Every gap becomes a numbered open question, not an invented requirement

- @Techniques@: keep the spec from drifting
  - Stop for a human approval before the implementation starts
  - Link the spec from the PR, so the reviewer checks the code against a
    written contract, not against the title of the issue

- @Remark@: the rule _"ask, do not guess"_ is the most important line of every
  planning skill
  - `project.write_prd`, `architecture.write_design`, `project.create_plan`,
    and `auto_task.criticize` all stop and wait when the input is unclear

* Tracing Requirements to PRs and Tests
// From: .claude/skills/project.create_plan/SKILL.md:81 '# Verification'
// From:

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Goal@: each requirement is built by some PR and checked by some test

- @Techniques@: the checks of `/project.create_plan`
  - Every PRD requirement is covered by at least one PR
  - Every requirement id in the plan links to its H3 in the PRD

- @Example@: `PR_A2` lists `R2` (alias format) and `D4` (reserved words)
  - Its tests check both: `my-talk` is accepted, and `My Talk!` and `health`
    are rejected

- @Key idea@: a requirement with no PR is forgotten, and a PR with no
  requirement is scope creep
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz[width=85%]
digraph Trace {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.30;
    ranksep=0.35;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.07",
          height=0.36];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // spec : orange | plan : teal | check : green

    R   [label="R2 in 01-prd.md",
         fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    D   [label="D4 in 03-decisions.md",
         fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Pr  [label="PR_A2 in 04-plan.md",
         fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    T   [label="Test_validate_alias",
         fillcolor="#A9DDB0", color="#4F9A5C", fontcolor="#1F4E2E"];

    R -> Pr [label="  Built by  "];
    D -> Pr [label="  Followed by  "];
    Pr -> T [label="  Checked by  "];
}
```
label=fig:trace
caption=One requirement and one decision (orange) traced to the plan entry (teal) and the test that checks it (green)

::::
:::

* Decisions Made While Planning
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:375 '## Record Every Non-Obvious Choice'
// From:

- @Motivation@: design and planning make most of the choices of a project, and
  the code records none of the reasons

- @Techniques@: record a decision in `03-decisions.md` when
  - The design or the plan picks one option among several
  - A design departs from an architecture rule
  - A PR description lists it under _Decisions_: `/project.update_progress`
    moves it to the log after the merge

- @Example@: `D5`: _"The rate limiter keeps its counters in memory"_
  - _Why_: no new service, and `v0` runs as one process
  - _Status_: superseded by `D9`, when the service ran two processes and a
    client got twice the limit

- @Remark@: a decision is never deleted: it is superseded, so the log keeps
  the reason why the old choice looked right

# Tracking Progress

* The Progress Log
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:329 '## Structure the Log File'
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:343 '## Write the Log'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=58%}
- @Definition@: `07-progress.md` records everything done, so a new person sees
  what happened and why without asking

- @Components@:
  - _Status_: one row per milestone
  - _Built_: one bullet per component
  - _Findings_: facts with a measured number
  - _Next_: the next tasks, from the plan
  - _History_: one row per date, never rewritten

- @Comparison@: the plan holds the future, the log the past
  - _"Will write the clicks in batches"_: plan
  - _"Batches cut the p95 to 6 ms"_: log
::::
:::: {.column width=42%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```text
  # Progress
  ## Status
  ## Built
  ## Findings
  1. <Fact>: <number, unit>
     - Data: <link>
  ## Next
  1. <Next task>
  ## History
  | Date | Done |
```
\endgroup

::::
:::

* Findings Need Evidence
// From:
// From: .claude/skills/project.rules.md:343 '## Write the Log'

- @Problem@: _"analytics are fast"_ is not a finding: nobody can check it, and
  nobody can tell when it stops being true

- @Solution@: each finding gives what was measured, where, and the number with
  its unit, including the negative results

// TODO(gp): The numbers of these findings are illustrative: replace them with
// the measured ones once the shortlink progress log exists
- @Example@: findings 2 and 3 of the `shortlink` features
  - Writing one click row in each redirect raised the p95 of `GET /{code}`
    from 4 ms to 31 ms, on a replay of 10,000 requests
  - Writing the click rows in a batch once per second: p95 of 6 ms
  - The cost: a crash loses up to 1 s of clicks

- @Interpretation@: the finding changed the code, since a redirect no longer
  writes to the database, and the decisions log, since `D10` accepts the loss
  of up to 1 s of clicks

- @Key idea@: an agent can write a log entry, but it must never invent a
  number: a missing number is a question for the human

* Closing the Loop After a Merge
// From: .claude/skills/project.update_progress/SKILL.md:21 '# Workflow'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:443 '## Update Docs and Close GH Issue'

- @Algorithm@: `/project.update_progress <PR_ID>`
  1. Check that the PR is merged, or stop: a PR is checked only when merged
  2. Tick `[x]` the PR in the roadmap and in its entry of `04-plan.md`
  3. Add the entry to `07-progress.md`: the issue, the author, the UTC dates,
     and the testing evidence
  4. Move each decision of the PR description to `03-decisions.md`
  5. Update the design docs of the changed modules and the index

- @Constraints@:
  - Append to the history: never rewrite a past row
  - Ask the user for a finding or a number that is not in the PR
  - Do not commit: the human commits the doc update in the PR that finishes the
    task

- @Key idea@: the merge is not the end of a task: the task ends when the plan
  and the logs say it is done

* When the Plan Meets an Agent
// From:

// TODO(gp): This history is an illustrative scenario: replace it with the
// real history once an agent runs the shortlink plan
- @Example@: an agent gets the plan and the design docs of `shortlink`
  - _Day 1_: `04-plan.md` has 4 versions and 14 scheduled PRs
  - _Day 2_: the code of `v0.1` to `v0.3` and its tests are written _in one
    pass_, from the design docs
  - _Days 2 to 5_: 4 new decisions (`D9` to `D12`) reshape the rate limiter
    and the click table

- @Problem@: one pass skipped the planned review
  - 12 PRs are _"written"_ on one branch and none is merged
  - No spec exists for the PRs marked `Spec: needed`

- @Solution@: the plan stays honest
  - A `Status:` line in each entry says _"written, not merged"_, so no box is
    ticked
  - The plan grows to 15 scheduled PRs, and each change has a dated row

- @Key idea@: an agent can outrun the plan in a day, so the plan must record
  what is written, what is reviewed, and what is merged as three states

# Planning With an AI

* Task Lists for Agents
// From: .claude/templates/auto_task.template.md:3 '### [ ] <Title of the GitHub Issue>'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/ai/all.auto_task.how_to_guide.md:60 '## Step 1: Create a Task List'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=50%}
- @Definition@: an **auto_task** is a unit of work that an agent completes end
  to end for one GitHub issue: a spec, branches, and PRs

- @Components@: the task template
  - _Problem_ before _Solution_
  - _Info_: confidence, complexity, and how to verify
  - _Solution_: one `PR<N>` block per PR

- @Techniques@: two sources of a task list
  - A plan PR, via `auto_task.create_plan_for_feature`
  - TODOs in the code, via `auto_task.create_specs_from_todos`
::::
:::: {.column width=50%}

\begingroup \scriptsize
```text
  ### [ ] <Issue title>
  Problem
  - <Problem and goal>
  Info
  - Type, reason
  - Confidence: low|med|high
  - Complexity: low|med|high
  - Verification plan
  Solution
  - [ ] PR1: <Goal>
    - <Change>
  - [ ] PR2: <Goal>
```
\endgroup

::::
:::

* A Queue of Tasks
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.A_queue_of_AI_coding_agents.md:43 '## Getting Tasks in the Queue'
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.how_to.A_queue_of_AI_coding_agents.md:119 '## Adding Tasks to the Queue'

- @Definition@: a **task queue** is one markdown file, e.g., `ai_task_queue.md`,
  with three sections: `Ready`, `Backlog`, and `Done`
  - Each task has a title, a problem, a solution, a complexity, and an
    importance

- @Algorithm@: grooming the queue
  1. Add a task to `Backlog` as it comes up
  2. Refine its spec and triage its importance over time
  3. Move it to `Ready` only when the spec is clear enough
  4. Create the issue, the branch, and the PR with one command, and hand it to
     an agent

- @Examples@: good candidates for the queue
  - `TODO(ai_gp)` comments, coverage gaps, lint warnings, one-off refactors
  - A tracer bullet: a one-shot prototype to see the impact of an idea

- @Remark@: a recurring task, e.g., docs in sync or coverage of the repo, runs
  on a schedule, not through the queue

* Criticize Before Executing
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.criticize/SKILL.md:6 '# Goal'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.rules.md:21 '## Ask for Clarification Before Executing an Unclear Plan'
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/ai/all.auto_task.how_to_guide.md:68 '## Step 2: Criticize the Plan'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Problem@: an agent that executes an unclear task guesses, and builds the
  next tasks on top of the guess

- @Solution@: `/auto_task.criticize` reads the plan before any execution, and
  checks that
  - The problem and the solution are clear and complete
  - Each affected repo is identified
  - Each clarification is asked before the work starts

- @Constraints@: if a task is not perfectly clear, the agent must not perform
  it: it writes a plan of 5 bullets and waits

- @Key idea@: criticizing costs one cheap call, and a wrong task costs a
  branch, a review, and a revert
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

```graphviz[width=65%]
digraph Criticize {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.35;
    ranksep=0.35;
    rankdir=TB;

    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.6,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, margin="0.12,0.07",
          height=0.36];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    // plan : teal | check : blue | human : rose | run : coral

    Tasks [label="tasks.md",
           fillcolor="#B7DDD0", color="#6FA890", fontcolor="#1F4E39"];
    Crit  [label="/auto_task.criticize",
           fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    Ask   [label="Ask the human,\nfix the spec",
           fillcolor="#F6E1E8", color="#D98CA8", fontcolor="#6B2A44"];
    Run   [label="Execute",
           fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];

    Tasks -> Crit;
    Crit -> Ask [label="  Unclear  "];
    Crit -> Run [label="  Clear  "];
    { rank=same; Ask; Run; }
    Ask -> Run [style=invis];
}
```
label=fig:criticize
caption=The criticize step (blue) sends a clear plan (teal) to execution (coral) and an unclear one back to the human (rose)

::::
:::

* Executing the Plan in a Loop
// From: helpers_root/docs/tools/ai/all.auto_task.how_to_guide.md:83 '## Step 3: Choose an Execution Mode'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.rules.md:48 '## Track Task Status'
// From: .claude/skills/auto_task.rules.md:55 '## The User Owns Commit and Merge Decisions'

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Mode", "How it runs", "Use it when"),
  rows: (
    (raw("execute_interactively"), "Execute, review, commit, one task at a time", "The task is exploratory and needs a tight loop"),
    (raw("execute_with_stacked_prs"), "Builds a stack of dependent branches and PRs without stopping", "The tasks form a chain and the specs are complete"),
    (raw("execute_remotely_with_single_pr"), "Dispatches the task to an agent on GitHub", "The task is clear and needs no local Docker or debugging"),
  ),
  caption: "Three ways to execute a task list, from most to least supervised",
  col-widths: (1.5fr, 1.8fr, 1.8fr),
)
```

- @Techniques@: track each task in the task list: `[ ]` not started, `[.]` in
  progress, `[x]` done

- @Constraints@: the agent may push to the branch of its task, but it never
  merges a PR: landing a change is always the human's call

- @Remark@: the permission policy is covered in Lesson 03.01, and stacked PRs
  in Lesson 14.01

* The Flow as Commands
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:326 '## Define'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:342 '## Plan'
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:356 '## Build'

\begingroup \scriptsize
```text
  > Define (paths are in <project>/docs/)
  claude> /project.write_prd         ../idea.md
  claude> /text.criticize            01-prd.md
  claude> /architecture.write_design 01-prd.md
  > Plan
  claude> /project.create_plan       02-design.architecture.md
  claude> /auto_task.criticize       04-plan.md
  claude> /auto_task.create_plan_for_feature 04-plan.md PR_A1
  claude> /auto_task.criticize       05-tasks.md
  > Build (choose one execution mode)
  claude> /auto_task.execute_interactively 05-tasks.md
  > Close (after the human merges)
  claude> /project.update_progress   PR_A1
```
\endgroup

- @Key idea@: each line is a skill, and between two lines a human reads the
  output and commits, so each skill gives its own diff to review

* Spec-Driven Tools
// From: website/docs/blog/posts/draft.survey.tools_to_generate_sw_with_agents.md:3 '## 1. Spec-driven planning and task tracking'

- @Motivation@: the flow of this lesson is one instance of a broader practice,
  **spec-driven development**: specify, plan, split into tasks, implement

```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Tool", "What it does"),
  rows: (
    ("GitHub Spec Kit", "Open-source toolkit: specify, plan, split into tasks, implement, with several agents"),
    ("Kiro", "An IDE built around specs: requirements, then design, then tasks"),
    ("OpenSpec", "A lightweight spec workflow to plan a change before the agent writes code"),
    ("BMAD Method", "Role-based agents (analyst, PM, architect, developer, QA) from idea to code"),
    ("Task Master", "Turns a PRD into tasks with dependencies that agents work through"),
  ),
  caption: "Spec-driven planning tools for coding agents",
  col-widths: (1fr, 3fr),
)
```

- @Comparison@: the do-it-yourself pattern of this repo
  - Specs and plans are markdown files in `docs/`, and each task is a GitHub
    issue
  - Pros: no new tool, and the docs are reviewed in PRs like code
  - Cons: the conventions live in rules and skills that the team maintains

* Limits of Planning With AI
// From: helpers_root/docs/planning/all.plan_work.how_to_guide.md:230 '## Create an End-to-End Plan'

- @Limitations@: what the flow does not cover yet
  - _Effort estimation_: the plan gives a complexity per PR, not hours or dates
  - _Roadmaps across quarters_: the plan covers one project, not a portfolio
  - _Plans of plans_: a large initiative needs several projects and their
    dependencies

- @Challenges@:
  - _Over-planning_: a detailed plan for a later version goes stale before it
    is built
  - _Plan drift_: the code changes faster than the plan when nobody runs the
    close step
  - _Agent speed_: an agent can build several versions before a human reviews
    the first one

- @Remark@: the answer to each challenge is the same, a small next step
  - Plan the next version in detail, review it, merge it, and log it

* Summary

- @Key idea@: code is cheap and understanding is not, so a human reviews a
  short plan before an agent writes a long diff
- Work flows from an idea to a project, tasks, and PRs, in four phases: define,
  plan, build, and close, each ended by a human gate
- A PRD says what to build with requirements that a test can check, non-goals,
  and success metrics, and never how to build it
- One plan file holds the roadmap and the PR entries: versions that run, PRs
  ordered by dependency, open questions, and the architecture rules by tag
- Every requirement traces to a PR and a test, and every non-obvious choice is
  a decision that is superseded, never deleted
- The progress log holds facts with evidence, and each merge closes the loop:
  tick the plan, log the result, record the decisions
- An agent executes a task list only after a criticize step finds the problem
  and the solution clear, and a human always merges

